OneiOS take19, userland/metal/patches/NativeMetal/0016: with oneios-drawable-release=1, an app's CAMetalLayer drawable counts
in use in the render server only while a command buffer reads it.
Upstream: none (OneiOS); 0003's commandUse (macOS-DroidVM 4f77b11's commit-to-completion rule) held to drawables.
Applies only after: 0003-surface-use-while-commands-read.patch
What: in backboardd, with ONEIOS_DRAWABLE_RELEASE=1, a texture over a surface named "CAMetalLayer Display Drawable"
(IOSurfaceCopyValue of kIOSurfaceName, "IOSurfaceName") is commandUse: its surface is IOSurfaceIncrementUseCount'ed at each
commit of a command buffer that reads it and decremented at that command buffer's completion, instead of counted for the
texture's life, in every oneios-surface-use mode. The name is asked only with the switch on (one IOSurface call for each
texture made over a surface).
Why: Maps (VectorKit, CAMetalLayer) logged "Failed to acquire drawable, rendering to temporary texture" 344 times in take18:
nextDrawable came back empty (allowsNextDrawableTimeout: after 1 s) and VectorKit drew offscreen and copied, the big globe
lag. iOS 27's QuartzCore (24A446 cache): get_unused_drawable (0x184530d2c) hands a presented drawable out again only while
IOSurfaceIsInUse(its surface) is false (0x184530dc8). Core Animation counts a drawable in use itself:
layer_private_present_impl calls CAImageQueueInsertImage_ (IOSurfaceIncrementUseCount), and the client's release_image
(IOSurfaceDecrementUseCount) runs once the render server gave the image back (layer_collectable_signal). It names the surface
in CA::SurfaceUtil::CAIOSurfaceCreate (IOSurfaceSetValue of kIOSurfaceName). The frontend counted the surface in use as well,
for the life of the render server's texture over it (the base's countUse, which 0003 keeps for every one-plane surface), and
that texture lives as long as Core Animation's image cache keeps it (CA::OGL::Context::lookup_image,
MetalContext::retain_image, freed by Context::collect), not as long as the drawable is shown: a drawable the render server
gave back can stay in use until the cache lets its texture go. That is read from the code, not measured; the A/B below
measures it. Counted from commit to completion, a drawable goes back to its layer once Core Animation released it and the
composition that read it completed, which is the GPU's part of Apple's count. The scanout presenter (GpuScanoutPresenter.h)
takes no part: it copies the render server's own framebuffer at the display driver's swaps; an app's drawable reaches the
screen through the render server's composition.
Risk: a drawable its app draws into again while the render server still samples it would tear or flicker. Core Animation's own
count covers it from its present until the render server gives it back, and the switch's count covers each command buffer that
reads it; a read encoded after the give-back and before its commit would not be covered (none is known). Not booted.
Switch: boot argument oneios-drawable-release=1 (ONEIOS_DRAWABLE_RELEASE=1); off by default: as before. Removable: yes
(ONEIOS_METAL_SKIP=drawables-in-use-while-read); left out with 0003.
iOS's log (backboardd, the switch on): "native-metal: CAMetalLayer drawable WxH 'BGRA': in use only while a command buffer
reads it (oneios-drawable-release=1)", the first four; the A/B is Maps' count of "Failed to acquire drawable" (344 in take18)
and its globe's frame rate.
